<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mwai Kibaki</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1931">1931</a>–<a href="/years/2022">2022</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mwai Kibaki was president of Kenya from 2002 to 2013. An economist, he served as finance minister under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jomo-kenyatta">Jomo Kenyatta</a> and vice-president under Daniel arap Moi before leading the opposition. His election in 2002 ended the long rule of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenya-african-national-union">KANU</a>; his disputed re-election in 2007 was followed by violence that killed over a thousand people.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Emilio Stanley Mwai Kibaki was born on 15 November 1931 in Gatuyaini village, Othaya division of the then Nyeri District, the youngest son of Kikuyu peasants Kibaki Gĩthĩnji and Teresia Wanjikũ. Italian missionaries baptised him Emilio Stanley, but he was known as Mwai Kibaki throughout his public life. He attended the Karima mission school and Mathari School, where he also learned carpentry and masonry, and at Mang'u High School from 1947 to 1950 he gained the highest grade in his O Level examinations.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In early 1960 Kibaki left his post at Makerere to become an executive officer of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenya-african-national-union">Kenya African National Union</a> (KANU), at the request of Thomas Joseph Mboya, and helped draft the independence constitution. In 1963 he was elected Member of Parliament for Doonholm in Nairobi and appointed Permanent Secretary for the Treasury. He became Minister of Commerce and Industry in 1966 and Minister of Finance and Economic Planning in 1969, and was Vice-President under Daniel arap Moi from 1978 to 1988.</p><p>He sat as an opposition member from 1992, ran unsuccessfully for the presidency in 1992 and 1997 and led the official opposition in Parliament from 1998 to 2002. For the 2002 election his Democratic Party joined other opposition parties in the National Alliance of Kenya, which merged with the Liberal Democratic Party to form the National Rainbow Coalition. On 14 October 2002 Raila Odinga declared him its candidate with the words &quot;Kibaki Tosha!&quot;.</p><p>Kibaki was sworn in on 30 December 2002, ending four decades of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenya-african-national-union">KANU</a> rule. His administration introduced free primary education in January 2003. In the referendum of 21 November 2005, 58 per cent of voters rejected a draft constitution he supported, and two days later he dismissed his entire cabinet. After the disputed election of 2007, in which he stood for the Party of National Unity against Odinga's ODM, mediation led by Kofi Annan produced a national accord in February 2008. Kibaki remained president, and on 17 April 2008 Odinga was sworn in as Prime Minister. A new constitution was promulgated in 2010. Kibaki handed the presidency to Uhuru Kenyatta on 9 April 2013 and died on 21 April 2022, aged 90.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mwai Kibaki” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Domestic and foreign pressure restored multi-party politics in 1991, and <b>Mwai Kibaki's</b> victory in 2002 ended KANU's rule.</blockquote><a href="/state/kenya">Kenya</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Gatuyaini, in what is now <a href="/countries/kenya">Kenya</a>. Died in Nairobi, in what is now <a href="/countries/kenya">Kenya</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-0.55,36.93&amp;year=1990" aria-label="Gatuyaini on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5576 2184 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Mwai Kibaki"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6026 2454h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6026 2454h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-0.55,36.93&amp;year=1990">What was here in 1990? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
